course: dictionary
lesson: 3936
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, so the words around it are your clue. «We bought a bag of chocolate fudge at the seaside.» Here fudge is a soft, sweet candy, because you buy it and eat it at the seaside.
explain: «Oh fudge!» said Sam when his cake fell on the floor. Fudge is not food here. It is a mild word people use instead of a swear word, so Sam is cross and upset, not hungry.
explain: Some meanings are about avoiding the truth. To fudge a question is to give no clear answer, and to fudge numbers is to change them a little so they look better. Good scientists never fudge their results, because that is cheating.
fact: Fudge is a soft, sweet candy made from sugar, butter and milk, often with chocolate.
fact: To fudge something means to avoid a clear answer, or to change facts or numbers a little to make them look better.
fact: "Oh fudge!" is a mild, polite way of saying a swear word when something goes wrong.
fact: "Fudging the results" in maths or science means cheating with numbers, which is wrong.
fact: A fudge can also be an unclear compromise that hides a problem instead of solving it.
exercise: «We bought a bag of chocolate fudge at the seaside.» What is fudge here? | A small wooden boat | A type of seaside bird | *A soft, sweet candy made from sugar and butter | Shouting loudly at the sea
exercise: Sam drops his cake and says «Oh fudge!» What does he mean? | He is asking for a piece of fudge | He is singing a song | He is hungry and wants more cake | *He is cross, using a mild word instead of a swear word
exercise: Ms Patel says the scientists must not fudge their results. What does she mean? | They should give their results to the newspapers | They must write their results in bigger letters | They should eat fudge while they work | *They must not change the numbers to cheat
test: A politician will not answer the question and tries to fudge it. What does fudge mean here? | To ask the question again, very slowly | *To avoid giving a clear answer | To sing a song loudly | To draw a picture of the question
test: «Drat», «blast», «sugar» and «fudge» are stand-ins for which kind of word? | *Swear words | Numbers | Sweet foods | Place names
exam: «He tried to fudge the numbers in his report.» What does fudge mean here? | Cook a sweet treat for his class | Give a loud cheer for the numbers | *Change the numbers a little to make them look better | Count the numbers very quickly
